artifact.self_contained_problem:
Background. A self-similar (or similarity) solution of a partial differential equation is a solution that is invariant under a one-parameter group of scaling transformations of the independent and dependent variables, so that, via a similarity variable combining the independent variables, the PDE is reduced to a problem in fewer independent variables. Such solutions frequently describe the intermediate-asymptotic behavior of solutions to wide classes of PDEs: namely, the behavior in regimes where the influence of fine details of initial and/or boundary data has been forgotten but the system has not yet reached its ultimate (e.g. equilibrium) state. Classical similarity solutions of the first kind have scaling exponents fixed by dimensional analysis; second-kind similarity solutions have exponents determined by nonlinear eigenvalue problems and are typically not detectable by dimensional analysis alone. Modern symmetry methods for differential equations include Lie point symmetries, contact and higher (generalized) symmetries, nonclassical and conditional symmetries, potential and nonlocal symmetries, equivalence transformations, and related group-theoretic constructions.

Problem. Develop modern symmetry approaches into a systematic, algorithmic family of symmetry-based techniques whose output, applied to a given partial differential equation (or class of PDEs) together with auxiliary conditions, is a list of candidate intermediate-asymptotic solutions describing the long-time, short-time, or far-field behavior of broad classes of solutions. More precisely, construct procedures that:

(i) take as input a PDE (or system) together with a specified asymptotic regime of interest;
(ii) produce, in finitely many constructive steps using symmetry-group machinery (Lie, generalized, nonclassical, potential, equivalence, etc.), a finite list of candidate self-similar or asymptotic ansätze, including those of the second kind with anomalous scaling exponents;
(iii) come with verifiable criteria guaranteeing that each candidate so produced actually captures the intermediate-asymptotic behavior of an identifiable class of initial/boundary value problems for the given PDE.

Determine to what extent such systematic symmetry-based methods can be formulated and proved to work, and identify the obstructions to a fully general procedure.
